{
  "id": "u10-l05",
  "unit": "u10-culture",
  "title": "Ekushe & national days",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "On **21 February 1952**, police in Dhaka opened fire on students and others demanding that **বাংলা** be a state language of Pakistan (which then included today's Bangladesh). Several were killed. Every year on **একুশে ফেব্রুয়ারি**, also called **শহীদ দিবস** (Martyrs' Day), people walk barefoot to the **শহীদ মিনার** from midnight on to lay flowers, singing *Amar bhaiyer rokte rangano Ekushe February* ('the 21st of February, reddened with my brother's blood'). Since 2000 the world also marks it as UNESCO's **International Mother Language Day** (**মাতৃভাষা** = mother tongue).\n\nTwo more national days mark the 1971 Liberation War. Both are public holidays (**ছুটি**):\n\n- **স্বাধীনতা দিবস**: Independence Day, **26 March**\n- **বিজয় দিবস**: Victory Day, **16 December**\n\n**দিবস** is the formal word for a named 'Day'; everyday 'day' is **দিন**. 'On' and 'to' take **-e**: **স্বাধীনতা দিবসে ছুটি** 'Independence Day is a holiday', **আমরা শহীদ মিনারে যাই** 'we go to the Shaheed Minar'.",
  "vocab": [
